\pard\sa200\sl276\slmult1\f0\fs22\lang9 In my study of The Grapes of Wrath, Ethan Frome and the Great Gatsby, I came to believe that American novels during this period sought to examine obsession within the American psyche. This is obvious in the areas of the American dream, which is the chief obsession and motivation of a large number of American characters. Scholar Frank McLynn wrote, \lquote American culture is characterised by permanent restlessness, marked by spatial mobility, relocation and the belief in the Fresh Start,\rquote  which aligns with the obsession with American dream ideals. Furthermore, romantic obsession is an important part in many American novels, being the primary motivation for idealistic characters who are ungrounded from reality. Another source of obsession is a sense of justice hard-baked into American ideals as deep as the constitution and addressed by many recent historical developments such as emancipation or the beginning of FDR\rquote s New Deal. \par
\par
 \par
\par
The American dream is presented as a primary psychological obsession of the characters throughout The Great Gatsby, The Grapes of Wrath, and further American literature I have read. The Joads\rquote  journey can be taken as a flight in pursuit of the American dream; they leave the \lquote scarred earth\rquote  of Oklahoma for the \lquote beautiful valley\rquote  of California at the end of Highway 66. This plays upon the idea presented by Frank McLynn; inherently, this is a restless journey in pursuit of something greater. An obsession with ambition and the American dream. The Joads' specific, unrealistic obsession with California is presented through most of the family. In chapter 10, Ma Joad, who is the central person holding the family together, talks about how in California it is \lquote Never cold\rquote  and there is \lquote fruit ever'place\rquote . This is an unrealistic expectation that ends up being false, and the California they are eventually presented with is difficult for the family, as work is unable to be found, and when it is found, it is paid extremely poorly. There is a sense that the American Dream ends up not existing for these characters despite their obsession with it. Further obsession with the American dream is shown by Connie, who is frequently obsessed by the idea of studying \lquote maybe radio\rquote  so he can become an expert and make a living off of it. Both Rose of Sharon and Connie become obsessed with their own perception of the American dream, constituted of living in a city and having good earnings and stable incomes, that it drives them to want to leave the family, who are obviously humbler. Eventually Connie himself would leave; he leaves behind his wife and unborn child due to his unhealthy and selfish obsession with success and material comfort that for him overrules familial connections. It is shown that in The Grapes of Wrath, people are willing to abandon family but also their old familial land; this is the initial objection of Grandpa Joad to leaving Oklahoma. He dies very shortly after leaving, and Jim Casy says, \lquote . Your way was fixed an' Grampa didn' have no part in it.\rquote  and that Grampa was \lquote jus' stayin' with the lan'. He couldn leave it\rquote . The Joads are willing to sacrifice their ancestral land due to their obsession with the American dream. In The Great Gatsby, this obsession with the American dream and societal progression is presented by the characters of Nick Carraway and Jay Gatsby, or James Gatz. These characters move east looking for prosperity; this eastward movement contrasts the westward trek of the Joads and reflects the changes to America in the decade between the novels. Both Gatsby and Carraway abandon their families, estranging themselves similarly to how Connie did, or Rose of Sharon was planning to do. While Nick's and Gatsby\rquote s obsession with the American dream is fuelled by individual motivation, they both share this life-changing and ruining obsession. Nick becomes an alcoholic who hates the people he was surrounded with, and Gatsby dies alone and has only three people attend his funeral. This obsession similarly damages and endangers life as it does in The Grapes of Wrath. Gatsby\rquote s meteoric rise to the top only leaves him lonely, surrounded by people exploiting him. Despite the economic gulf between the characters of the two novels, they both pursue societal climb and to leave their past behind them; it's just that Nick and Gatsby are more successful. These characters are willing to sacrifice up to their own lives for the American dream. In Ethan Frome it is repeatedly discussed how the eponymous Ethan Frome wished to \lquote be an engineer and to live in towns, where there were lectures and big libraries\rquote ; this can be seen as a further wish to pursue the American dream. Frome wishes to socially progress, economically too, and is instead trapped by his wife Zeena and her poor health. He is obsessed and repeatedly mentions this idea of pursuing his own idealised form of the American dream in the form of the engineering job. He would try to escape his own life by using his relationship with Mattie, and this eventually leads to him gravely injuring himself. Again, a character is willing to harm themselves in pursuit of their American dream, showcasing their obsession. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Love is another obsession of characters throughout works I have read. In Ethan Frome, the story revolves around Ethan\rquote s romantic infatuation with Mattie Silver. His love fuels him and is everything he surrounds his life in. It fuels his hope in escaping his stark and miserable surroundings. The warmth he feels from Mattie is strongly contrasted with the off-putting coldness of Zeena. Ethan projects his dreams and hopes of escape onto Mattie and becomes hopeless again once she is sent away by Zeena near the end of the novel. He ignores the reality of his marriage with her and the rest of his life in Starkfield. Further, the cold and bleak landscape of Frome\rquote s Starkfield parallels and represents his emotional isolation; the graves of his family are frequently mentioned, and the farmhouses surrounding him are described as \ldblquote mute and cold as a gravestone". Mattie is the only person he interacts with outside of some business partners and customers and his wife Zeena, with whom he has a highly strained relationship, so it is inevitable that their relationship becomes obsessive. Similarly, Gatsby\rquote s love for Daisy is deeply obsessive and impossible. However, Gatsby\rquote s love is grand and idealistic rather than driven by a kinship built by situation. To Gatsby, Daisy represents higher-class life, and he is willing to even go into criminal enterprise to try and entertain her. Gatsby arguably naively decides to throw extravagant and absurdly expensive parties as a way to try and lure her out. Gatsby even uses Nick as a way to contact her, as he is not able to contact her from over the \ldblquote sound\rdblquote  due to the class differences. For both Frome and Gatsby, the love they obsessively change is different from the reality they arrive upon. For both Gatsby and Frome, the love they obsess over gives tragic consequences; for Frome, Mattie becomes more similar to the Zeena he tries to escape, and for Gatsby, he loses his life. Both characters obsessiveness surrounding love blinds them to their situation, which could be a comment on the dangers of obsession and idealising a person. In both American novels, obsession is key to the central and eponymous characters, who both systematically degrade and destroy themselves through love. \par
\par
 \par
\par
In American novels there is a continued obsession with justice or righting wrongs or meaning through pursuit of goals. In Moby Dick this is shown by Ahab\rquote s continued pursual of his white whale, Moby Dick. Ahab\rquote s obsession is endangering to himself and his crewmates; he loses his legs, and this causes him to fixate. He seeks to try and right the wrongs done to him by Moby Dick obsessively. This goes beyond a healthy quest and turns into personal vengeance. This could suggest that American novels in this period sought to evaluate the ways justice was hard-baked into the American psyche and thus were obsessive topics. Similarly, in The Grapes of Wrath, Jim Casy becomes involved in union work and, due to his personal philosophy, obsessively pursues it. He expresses radical ideas that all people are equal and deserve fair treatment and eventually loses his life, becoming a martyr for the cause. It is arguable that it is reasonable to go this far for justice when you view it as important as these characters do. Casy, since leaving the church, had been using progressive ideals and only found a movement to filter them through and let his obsession grow into a powerful movement. Casy is another example of a character who is willing to risk life and death for his obsession. Tom Buchanan is willing to pursue his own wrongful ideas of justice consistently throughout the novel as he focuses on his own racial prejudice and a pursuit of perverse justice against Gatsby. Tom, early in the novel, talks about \lquote The Rise of the Coloured Empires\rquote , describing it as \lquote scientific stuff\rquote , suggesting he believes it. He also acts chauvinistically, accusing Daisy and Jordan of \lquote running around\rquote  too much. He is obsessed with perverse ideas of justice and social order. He enacts his revenge on Gatsby by angering Wilson and causing Gatsby\rquote s demise; this shows his obsessiveness and his inability to forgive or forget what he and Daisy did. Overall, these characters obsess themselves with their visions of retributive justice and social order. \par
\par
 \par
\par
Overall, characters in the American novels I have studied frequently obsess over things, and this is often the defining state of the characters. The American dream and the restless and reckless actions associated with it are primary obsessions of many characters. Additionally, characters are often driven by romantic obsession, and this further drives them to reckless action. Characters are often also driven by obsession with justice, especially the retributive kind. These are all often taken to the point of recklessness or life-threatening, and this could be commentary on the American disposition to restlessness. \par
